\label{def:Stability_Definitions}
A point $\bm{a} \in \mathcal{X}$ s.t. $\bm{f}(\bm{a}) = \bm{0}$ of the system $\dot{\bm{x}} = \bm{f}(\bm{x})$, is called:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Locally Stable: if for any $\varepsilon > 0$, there exists a $\delta > 0$ such that if $\norm{\bm{x}(0) - \bm{a}}_2 < \delta$, then $\norm{\bm{x}(t) - \bm{a}}_2 < \varepsilon$ for all $t \ge 0$.

    \item Locally Asymptotically Stable: if it is locally stable and, in addition, it is locally attractive, i.e., there exists a $\delta_a > 0$ such that if $\norm{\bm{x}(0) - \bm{a}}_2 < \delta_a$, then $\lim_{t \to \infty} \bm{x}(t) = \bm{a}$.
\end{enumerate}
The set of initial states $\bm{x}(0)$ that converge to $\bm{a}$ as $t \to \infty$ is the region of attraction $\mathrm{ROA}(\bm a)$. Any set $S \subset$ $\mathrm{ROA}(\bm a)$ such that $\bm a \in S$ is called an inner approximation of $\mathrm{ROA}(\bm a)$.

\label{def:local_LF}
A $\mathcal{C}^1$ function $V: \Omega \mapsto \mathbb{R}$, defined on an open set $\Omega \subset \mathcal{X}$ containing the equilibrium point $\bm{a}$, is a local Lyapunov function if:
\begin{enumerate}
    \item Positive Definiteness: $V(\bm{a}) \!=\! 0$, $V(\bm{x})\! > \!0,\forall \bm{x}\! \in\! \Omega \!\setminus \!\{\bm{a}\}$.
    \item Lie-derivative Negative Semidefiniteness: $\mathcal{L}_f V(\bm{x}) \!=\! \nabla V(\bm{x})^{\!\top}\! \bm{f}(\bm{x})$, is negative semidefinite, $\mathcal{L}_f V(\bm{x})\! \le\! 0,\forall\bm{x}\!\in \!\Omega$.
\end{enumerate}
If the Lie-derivative is negative definite (i.e., $\mathcal{L}_f V(\bm{x}) < 0$ for all $\bm{x} \in \Omega \setminus \{\bm{a}\}$), $V$ is a strict local Lyapunov function.

\label{def:Taylor_polynomial}
Let $\mathcal X\subset\mathbb R^{n}$ be an open set, and let the vector field $\bm f:\mathcal X\mapsto\mathbb R^{n}$ be of class $\mathcal C^{p+1}$ for some $p\ge0$. For a fixed point $\bm a\!\in\!\mathcal X$ and a displacement $\bm h=\bm x-\bm a \in\mathbb R^{n}$ such that the line segment $\{\bm a+\theta\bm h:0\le \theta\le1\}$ is contained in $\mathcal X$ (e.g., if $\mathcal X$ is convex, $\bm a \!\in\! \mathcal{X}$ and $\bm a +\bm h \!\in\! \mathcal X$ suffice), the $p$th-order Taylor polynomial of $\bm f$ centered at $\bm a$ is defined as
\begin{equation}\label{eq:taylor_poly}
    \bm f_p(\bm a+\bm h)=\sum_{k=0}^{p}\frac1{k!}\,
    \nabla^{\,k}\bm f(\bm a)(\bm h)^{(k)}.
\end{equation}
